Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, President, and thank you, High Commissioner, for your briefing.
The United States remains committed to constructively contributing to efforts to achieve peace and end displacement by fostering the conditions for refugees to return home.
For example, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, the United States brokered the Washington Accords, which allowed displaced refugees to return home. The parties must demonstrate a continued, and earnest commitment to this political process.
For Sudan, the United States continues to advocate for a humanitarian truce and calls on parties to protect civilians and negotiate in good faith. Humanitarian assistance must be able to reach those in need.
We have also renewed our efforts towards a swift and durable end to the Russia-Ukraine war through ceasefire and negotiated settlement. We continue to support the return of all civilians, especially children, who have been displaced or forcibly transferred as a result of this conflict.
We must not forget about the too-often overlooked displacement crises, including the reality facing the Sahrawi refugees. There is a window of opportunity to end this protracted political dispute and give these refugees a chance at a dignified and free future. We call on all Member States and stakeholders to join us in making this opportunity a reality.
In all these cases, the United States firmly believes that deterring and ending displacement – rather than just managing it year after year – must be the goal.
In FY2025, the United States contributed more than $1.4 billion in humanitarian assistance to UNHCR, UNICEF, and IOM. These funds helped to address refugee crises, internal displacement, and disasters around the world, including Sudan, DRC, and Ukraine. The United States has also additionally provided billions of dollars through the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs pooled funds, most of which supports UNHCR activities.
The international system, though, cannot continue to treat forced displacement as a burden for only host and donor states. Origin-country actors must finally face accountability. Responsibility for these problems cannot continue to be quietly transferred to the rest of the world.
Governments in countries of origin have a part to play and we must hold them accountable and to a higher standard. They must create conditions for returns and promptly accept their returned nationals.
We also believe that the UNHCR has a key role to play in fostering refugee returns. The United States supports the High Commissioner’s “50 by 35” vision where it reflects a genuine pivot toward solutions and self-reliance. We urge UNHCR to remain at the forefront of these worldwide efforts to support refugees, so that they may finally return home.
The global refugee and asylum system, however, cannot be sustained without significant reforms. UNHCR’s most urgent priority should be to advance changes that halt the abuse of asylum mechanisms by economic migrants – perhaps the most powerful driver of mass migration.
We look forward to the long-overdue reforms to uphold national sovereignty and eliminate incentives for fraudulent abuses of the international protection system.
Thank you.
