Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, President. Thank you, Secretary-General Guterres, for your briefing. And of course, a warm welcome to the Foreign Minister of Panama.
The United States welcomes this debate and the opportunity to offer our reflections on the “Review on the Future of All Forms of United Nations Peace Operations.”
The United States is a leader in stopping conflicts and forging peace deals. We believe that decisive diplomacy, backed by real leverage, is what actually ends wars.
The United Nations, by contrast, is mired by bureaucracy and process – including lengthy reviews and statements – when addressing conflicts actually requires timely, concrete adaptation. Peacekeeping is no exception.
This review, however, does little to address current conflict realities or adapt peacekeeping. Successful peacekeeping demands clear mandates, real accountability, and articulated benchmarks to work towards – not indefinite mandate renewals that substitute for strategy.
If I can put this another way, the work we do here in New York must be worthy of the brave women and men working in the field, the civilians they work to protect, and of course the taxpayers, the hardworking taxpayers who make all of this work possible.
So, let me be direct: We have lost confidence that peacekeeping, as we currently design and resource it, delivers results proportionate to its cost and footprint. We must rigorously assess missions and hold them accountable for defined outcomes.
The United States therefore seeks reform organized around five principles:
First, the Secretariat and mission leadership must provide unvarnished feedback to this Council. The review itself repeats – as our colleague from Liberia pointed out – the review itself repeats the Brahimi report’s central assertion: the Secretariat must tell the Council what it needs to know, not what it wants to hear.
Candor gives Council members the ability to adapt mission priorities as circumstances evolve. This candor includes specific recommendations from the Secretariat so the Council can effectively reconfigure or terminate underperforming missions when needed.
For example, recently, the Secretariat produced a report that was supposed to detail plans for a mission’s transition and drawdown. Instead, what we received detailed very little. We asked for tangible plans, not prose about process.
Second, the Secretariat must develop genuine integrated, mission-level planning. We are pleased to see other countries agree on this, as well as on the importance of establishing a strategic vision and viable end state at a mission’s inception. Missions need to have realistic and achievable goals and, as the review recommends, we must be willing to terminate missions that fail to adapt.
Third, mandates must stay focused on security functions in service to a political plan, as highlighted in the Secretary-General’s recommendations in the review. The Council must stop adding superfluous language to resolutions that lead to lengthy, unfocused mandates. These efforts veer missions off course by miring them in political niceties instead of focusing them on core efforts.
Fourth, the review makes a critical point regarding the need to enhance accountability for performance and conduct. The Council must collectively demand that all stakeholders in peacekeeping uphold high performance standards and address misconduct. This includes the rigorous application of the zero-tolerance policy for sexual exploitation and abuse.
Deployment eligibility and continued participation in the mission must hinge on these standards. We expect the Secretariat and all contributing countries to match rhetoric with action.
Fifth, we demand continued strategic cost efficiencies across UN peacekeeping, especially where cuts do not undermine a mission’s ability to implement its mandate. The UN80 Initiative savings that this review cites are a starting point, but not a finish line.
Good stewardship of U.S. taxpayer dollars requires greater efficiency and accountability in how the Secretariat manages peacekeeping funds. Our support depends on demonstrated results and responsible use of assessed contributions. When missions are stretched thin, that is generally a function of poor prioritization, not insufficient resources.
So, let there be no ambiguity: peacekeeping has grown too big and too broad; it is trying to do too much. Peacekeeping missions, as currently constituted, are not unqualified successes. Rather, they are flawed instruments in need of fundamental reform.
We call on this Council to match our resolve and restore peacekeeping to its foundational purpose of maintaining international peace and security and to do so with the urgency that this moment demands.
I thank you.
