Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
Mr. President, we are not surprised by the actions of several Council members in vetoing the extension of the mandate of the Panel of Experts.
That’s because this Panel would have shined the light on the UNSCR-prohibited bilateral defensive cooperation between Iran and its partners.
Given this veto, it is clear that this is part of a broader effort to prevent appointments or renew mandates of expert panels to stifle reporting that implicates their own activities in support of rogue regimes in defiance of their UNSCR obligations.
Panels have learned the hard way that calling out members of this body for sanctions violations or evasion can have serious and sometimes tragic consequences.
This includes delays or refusals to approve individual experts for Yemen and the Central African Republic panels. In more extreme cases, entire expert panels have been eliminated, including those for Mali and DPRK. Mali’s sanctions regime itself was terminated.
Without an independent Panel of Experts, this Council loses its principal source of impartial, evidence-based reporting on sanctions violations—creating a monitoring gap that only benefits the Iranian regime and those who profit from evading those measures.
In view of this latest act of obstructionism, the United States will not cease efforts within this Committee to establish a Panel of Experts that befits the mandate of this Committee, as well as name a Committee chair and produce and post an updated sanctions list.
The United States looks forward to working with Member States on continuing the important work of this Committee.
Thank you, Mr. President.
