Ambassador Tammy Bruce
Deputy Representative of the United States to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, and thank you to today’s briefers.
The United States welcomes the ongoing progress that the Syrian authorities continue to make on political, humanitarian, and security priorities.
As we noted last month, the United States under President Trump is committed to supporting a stable, unified Syria, and to building a more prosperous and interconnected region.
We welcome the IAEA Board’s decision on September 9 to end its longstanding noncompliance finding for Syria.
It reflects the government’s commitment to cooperative engagement with the IAEA, and to transparency about the former regime’s clandestine nuclear program, and to the peaceful use of nuclear technology going forward.
We encourage Syria to continue working with the IAEA to strengthen its domestic safeguards of nuclear material, including by adopting an additional protocol and implementing its provisions in full. The conclusion of an additional protocol would be an important demonstration of the Syrian government’s enduring commitment to transparency and to fostering international trust.
We also take this opportunity to remind Security Council members that 15 years ago – on June 2011 – the Council first received notice from the IAEA that the Assad regime had violated its safeguards agreement.
As we said at the time, it was obvious that Assad had tried to build a nuclear reactor in order to produce weapons-grade plutonium. And yet some members of this Council shamelessly parroted the Assad regime’s lies and tried to deflect blame onto Israel.
Colleagues, we now know that all along the IAEA was right. Israel was right. The United States was right. We point this out not to boast, but to remind fellow Council members – and proliferator states like Iran – that with enough time and effort, the truth will always prevail.
As we did last month, the United States commends the Syrian Democratic Forces’ integration into Syrian state institutions, marking further progress toward Syria’s path to one army, one government, and one unified state.
We remain concerned, however, by the escalation in Suwayda, and reaffirm our support for the trilateral roadmap announced nearly a year ago by the United States, Syria, and Jordan – a path toward the dignified reintegration of the province and full rights and obligations of citizenship for its Druze community. We urge all parties to exercise restraint and pursue dialogue in earnest, and we look for continued progress toward accountability, security, and the safe return of the displaced.
We also call on both Israel and Syria to exercise restraint and seek de-escalation in regard to territorial issues. The United States will continue to engage closely with both countries and with all regional stakeholders to promote dialogue and peaceful solutions.
As we look ahead to UNGA high-level week next week, we encourage members of this Council and all Member States to use this opportunity for meaningful engagement with the Syrian government in support of a stable, secure, and prosperous Syria which is of course in the interest of the entire globe.
We reiterate our call for UN institutions and Member States to support the Syrian government’s efforts to advance these goals. We urge the Syrian government to work with the International, Impartial and Independent Mechanism (IIIM) and the Independent Institution on Missing Persons in Syria (IIMP) to finalize MOUs that will enable them to provide resources to support the ongoing transitional justice project in Syria.
We also take note of the special envoys and of the Under-Secretary-General of their remarks regarding the role of women in Syria, their ideas, their work and insight of women is key to the stability and legitimacy of the new Syria. Their role in every sector is important and is clear in the search for the missing. Their vital role must be fostered in every dynamic.
Thank you, Mr. President.
