AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. And thank you to the Assistant Secretary-General for your briefing.
The situation in Yemen has further deteriorated since the Council met just five days ago to discuss the situation.
Houthi forces have advanced along Yemen’s Red Sea coastline, extending their presence near the Bab al-Mandeb Strait, through which roughly 12 percent of global trade passes. We are monitoring these developments closely with our regional partners.
Houthi escalation has taken a terrible toll on civilians. The Houthis have bombed cities, displaced nearly 100,000 Yemenis, and killed a significant number of civilians. The Houthis have also destroyed tens of millions of dollars in vital port infrastructure which the region relies on for imports of food and other essential humanitarian goods.
The Houthis also continue to unjustly detain at least 20 of our local embassy staff and more than 70 UN local staff. The United States calls for their immediate and unconditional release.
Beyond their disregard for Yemeni civilians, Houthi actions threaten regional and global energy security. This includes attacks on multiple tankers and the killing of at least three mariners in the process. As the Government of Yemen has noted, threats to shipping raise transportation, insurance, food, fuel, and medicine costs worldwide.
The Houthis’ acts of terrorism also undermine regional stability. On September 14 and 15 the Houthis launched missile and drone attacks at multiple Saudi cities, wounding 13 civilians. This follows their September 8 attack, which wounded more than 70, including women and children, and ignited fires at critical oil facilities.
These attacks are only the latest example of the Houthis’ blatant disregard of Saudi sovereignty and civilian life.
As President Trump has emphasized, freedom of navigation underpins the global economy, and the United States is working with our partners to protect it.
The United States supports Yemen’s legitimate government as it counters the Iran-backed Houthi militia. We demand that the Houthis halt their advances, withdraw from seized territory, and cease attacks. We likewise demand that the Islamic Republic of Iran cease its support for the Houthis’ terrorist activities.
This Council must remain clear-eyed about Iran’s role in sustaining this conflict through arming, funding, and directing the Houthis. Addressing this external support is essential to eliminating Houthi capabilities.
We join the Republic of Yemen in calling on this Council to ensure effective implementation of Resolutions 2140 and 2216, and to halt the flow of arms and related materiel, as well as related technical assistance, training, and financial or other assistance to the Houthis. The Iranian regime is the principal violator of both resolutions. It has defied the arms embargo under Resolution 2216 and undermined the sanctions regime under Resolution 2140. This Council cannot look away from those facts.
The United States will continue to work with our partners to address this situation and support long-term regional stability.
Thank you.
