Jenifer Neidhart
Political Minister Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. I appreciated your briefing today, Special Envoy Grundberg. Welcome to the representatives of Yemen and Saudi Arabia in their joining this important meeting today.
On September 8, the Houthis launched missile and drone attacks against four Saudi cities, wounding more than 70 civilians, including women and children, and igniting fires at critical oil facilities.
The September 8 attack is only the latest example of the Houthis’ blatant disregard of Saudi sovereignty and civilian life. In recent months, they have bombed civilian infrastructure in Saudi Arabia and attacked maritime vessels – wounding dozens more.
These are the same tactics the Houthis are using to intimidate their fellow Yemenis. The Houthis have bombed cities, displacing thousands of families, while killing an indeterminate number of Yemeni civilians. Their strikes on Mokha have destroyed tens of millions of dollars of vital port infrastructure in Yemen.
These strikes represent flagrant violations of Saudi Arabia’s and Yemen’s sovereignty, and they pose a direct threat to regional and global energy security.
The pattern is unmistakable. The Houthis are agents and tools of Iran, and the Iranian hand behind these attacks is clear.
These attacks are part of a broader Houthi campaign to disrupt vital corridors, including the Bab al-Mandeb Strait, threatening navigational rights and freedoms of global commerce.
The United States stands firmly with the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia and reaffirms the Kingdom’s right to defend its territory and citizens. We also stand with the Republic of Yemen in its efforts to end Houthi terrorism and bring peace and stability to the Yemeni people.
We demand the Houthis immediately cease all attacks against Saudi Arabia, Yemen’s legitimate government, and international shipping.
We also draw the Council’s attention to the Houthis’ continued unjust detention of UN staff, humanitarian workers, and diplomatic personnel including staff of the U.S. Embassy in Sana’a and reiterate our demand for their immediate and unconditional release.
The path forward requires depriving the Houthis of the resources that sustain their acts of terrorism, including through stronger implementation of resolutions to which this Council has already agreed.
The Council must speak with one voice. This escalation is unacceptable, and those who enable it bear responsibility for its consequences.
The United States will continue working with our partners to hold the Houthis accountable and restore stability to this vital region. Thank you.
