Ambassador Tammy Bruce
Deputy Representative of the United States to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, and congratulations on this coming month. It’ll be busy. Looking forward to your work and France’s leadership. And for Ms. Nakamitsu, thank you for your informative briefing, and Mr. Ambassador, for your remarks.
Since our last meeting on this topic in June, the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) Executive Council (EC) has acknowledged Syria’s significant progress in tackling the remnants of the Assad regime’s chemical weapons program.
The United States joined other Executive Council (EC) members in approving, by consensus, the historic decision to reinstate Syria’s rights and privileges under the Chemical Weapons Convention. These rights had been suspended since 2021 in the context of the former regime’s use of chemical weapons.
During the same EC session, we also welcomed Syria’s submission of a new amendment to its initial declaration.
As reported by the OPCW Director-General, Syria has declared an additional chemical weapons production facility, a newly discovered chemical used in the production of nerve agents, and empty chemical munitions. It has also declared two chemical weapons storage facilities for which the EC approved facility agreements, and where newly discovered materials are stored while awaiting destruction.
This progress could not have been achieved without Syria and the OPCW Secretariat deploying significant technical, financial, and human resources.
They demonstrated unwavering determination in tracking down remnants of the Assad regime’s program, often under challenging conditions.
The United States is grateful to all members of the Syria-led Breath of Freedom Task Force for supporting this effort. We urge Syria to continue its close coordination with the Task Force, notably on technical steps needed for the success of this mission.
The next phase in this process is the verified and complete destruction of the identified remnants. As we speak, OPCW inspectors are deployed to Syria to verify the mechanical destruction of ‘Category three’ chemical weapons. Namely, empty M4000 aerial bombs and Volcano rockets similar to those used to deliver deadly chemical payloads on Khan Shaykhun in 2017 and Ghouta in 2013 respectively.
We look forward to the OPCW reporting on this first destruction operation since the 2013-2014 destruction of declared CW stockpiles.
The United States expresses appreciation to all who contributed to the Trust Fund for Syria missions. According to the OPCW Director-General’s August report, the Fund now covers expected expenses for 2026 and 2027 operations. We now encourage UN Member States to consider direct support to the Syrian government’s counter-CW efforts as Syria seeks to build its national capabilities for a future free of chemical weapons.
We also encourage Member States to assist Syria as it brings to justice those responsible for the barbaric use of chemical weapons.
As we reflect on Syria’s remarkable progress to date, we must also pause to remember the victims and survivors of the Assad regime’s chemical attacks, including the victims of the Ghouta attack–the anniversary of which we observed last month.
Our work to eliminate the Assad regime’s chemical weapons program and to promote accountability is not just essential for international peace and security: it is also a way to honor the Syrian men, women, and children whose lives were taken. Let their memory stir us onward as we work toward a Syria, and a world, finally free of chemical weapons.
Thank you.
